                {
                    "file": "tests/unit/dsp/pdr.cpp",
                    "line": 2071,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "Why 3 and 5?\n\nAlso, this loop makes it slightly irritating to understand which case failed, if a failure occurs. I prefer we avoid it - we could extract the loop body to a function and then invoke the function with necessary constants."
                },
                {
                    "file": "tests/unit/dsp/pdr.cpp",
                    "line": 2071,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "garybeihl",
                        "email": "garybeihl@microsoft.com",
                        "username": "garybeihl"
                    },
                    "message": "Extracted the loop body to checkExtractValidChildren(n) and call it with the\ntwo counts, with a SCOPED_TRACE(\"num_children=\" + ...) so the failing case is\nidentifiable. The values themselves aren't boundary cases \u2014 just two\nrepresentative valid child counts (a small one and a larger one needing a\nbigger allocation); added a comment saying so."
                },
                {
                    "file": "tests/unit/dsp/pdr.cpp",
                    "line": 2075,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Andrew Jeffery",
                        "email": "andrew@codeconstruct.com.au",
                        "username": "amboar"
                    },
                    "message": "Please use placement-new instead."
                },
                {
